6425977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425977 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ ${x}^{2}<9$\n\nwhere the universal set is $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0237c8ad-eb27-521a-9b96-cd671ad62b96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425977 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{x\\in U\\,|\\,P(x)\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U=R.$\n\nWe need to find $x\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}^{2}<9$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <9 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & <3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\n-3\\text{ }<x<3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is $T=(-3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425978 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425978 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425978 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425978 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ $5x+2>22,$\n\nwhere the universal set is $R.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10a98313-2524-5ce8-a6f0-176526143e93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425978 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{x\\in U\\,|\\,P(x)\\}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $x\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $5x+2>22$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U=R.$\n\nNow, we need to solve the above inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2>22 \\\\ 5x>20 \\\\ x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT=(4,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425979 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ $x+y\\le 0,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "713f4e9c-4d96-5c9b-be62-26529dd25eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425979 #edb/ref 1074 "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $x+y\\le 0$\n\nNow, we convert the inequality into the slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & \\le 0 \\\\ y & \\le -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](images/1e/1e8f03a8b229a8b2e46af33c700dec57a8abed693a58c75b6312e2ae5a6856c1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425980 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $P(y):$ ${y}^{2}-1=8$\n\nII. $Q(a,b):$ ${a}^{2}+{b}^{2}$\n\nIII. $R(x,y):$ $\\frac{x}{y}\\in N\\cap Q$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86866e7c-73c9-5409-8743-b71ad4d3cbe4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425980 #edb/ref 1074 "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(a,b)$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
